# 3. TECHNICAL SEO

Audit and implement the technical foundation.

## 3.1 Search engine accessibility

Ensure:
- Search engines can access important public pages.
- Important content is available in crawlable HTML.
- Server-side rendering or static generation is used appropriately.
- JavaScript does not unnecessarily hide important content from crawlers.
- Internal links use crawlable anchor elements.
- Important pages do not accidentally contain noindex directives.
- Canonical URLs are correctly configured.
- Redirects are handled correctly.
- Broken internal links are identified and fixed.

For Next.js, use the appropriate metadata API and rendering strategies.

## 3.2 URL architecture

Use clean, descriptive URLs.

Examples:

/ 
/about
/services
/developments
/developments/christos-gardens
/developments/havilah-city
/investment-opportunities
/contact
/blog
/blog/land-investment-in-ogun-state

Avoid:
- Random identifiers in public URLs.
- Unnecessary query parameters.
- Duplicate URL variations.
- Excessively deep URL structures.
- Keyword stuffing in URLs.

Implement permanent redirects when URLs change.

## 3.3 XML sitemaps

Build dynamic XML sitemaps.

Include:
- Homepage.
- About page.
- Services pages.
- Development directory.
- Individual development pages.
- Published blog articles.
- Other important public landing pages.

Requirements:
- Automatically include newly published public content.
- Exclude admin routes.
- Exclude draft content.
- Exclude private or authenticated pages.
- Exclude noindex pages.
- Use canonical URLs.
- Update when content changes.

Implement a sitemap index if the content grows sufficiently large.

Do not use arbitrary lastmod values. Use actual content modification timestamps.

## 3.4 Robots.txt

Generate a valid robots.txt.

Allow crawling of public pages and assets.

Disallow sensitive administrative areas, authentication pages and internal application routes where appropriate.

Reference the XML sitemap.

Do not accidentally block CSS, JavaScript or essential assets needed for rendering.

Robots.txt must not be treated as a security mechanism.

## 3.5 Canonicalisation

Every indexable page must have a correct canonical URL.

Handle:
- Trailing slashes consistently.
- HTTP to HTTPS.
- WWW versus non-WWW.
- Duplicate parameters.
- Pagination.
- Filtered property pages.
- Duplicate content.

Canonical URLs must point to the preferred public version.

## 3.6 Performance and Core Web Vitals

Optimise:
- Largest Contentful Paint (LCP).
- Interaction to Next Paint (INP).
- Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS).

Target Google's recommended thresholds:

- LCP: 2.5 seconds or less.
- INP: 200 milliseconds or less.
- CLS: 0.1 or less.

Implement:
- Image optimisation.
- Responsive image sizes.
- Lazy loading below the fold.
- Priority loading for the main hero image.
- Font optimisation.
- Code splitting.
- Reduced unnecessary JavaScript.
- Caching.
- Compression.
- Efficient database queries.
- Appropriate server-side rendering.
- Minimal layout shifts.

Do not lazy-load the primary above-the-fold hero image.

Do not sacrifice accessibility or functionality for performance scores.

